{"claim_id":"CL-01","claim":"The largest was 278 feet by 136 feet, weighed 3,320 tons, and was surveyed into position to a three-quarter-inch tolerance.[^1]","footnote_id":"1","citation":"Walsh Group / Archer Western–Turner–Phillips May–H.J. Russell (Innovation Next+ JV) release on Terminal F module set, August 2026: six mega-modules placed via self-propelled modular transporter across a closed taxiway over an approximately two-week overnight window; largest module 278 ft × 136 ft at 3,320 tons, set to ~¾-inch survey tolerance. Module dimensions and weight confirmed by Dallas Innovates, \"DFW Airport's Terminal F rises with record-setting modular construction and a scaled-up $4B plan to meet global demand,\" 2025. The ¾-inch survey tolerance corroborated by International Airport Review, August 2026.","evidence_ids":["infrastructure-economist::ev-56a69208d46e"],"retained":true,"primary_source_checked":true,"verification_status":"verified","verification_note":"Module dimensions (278 ft × 136 ft, 3,320 tons) confirmed by Dallas Innovates article (primary source). Survey tolerance (~¾ inch) corroborated by International Airport Review August 2026 per evidence ledger. The JV evidence IDs from the original lineage were replaced with the single infrastructure-economist ID that directly maps to the Dallas Innovates primary source.","draft_sha256":"f4164662caec14566df9aee233bdc31847bf118247ae098f2a7a5c4af5ea42f7"}
{"claim_id":"CL-02","claim":"Three anchors: a 37,000-square-foot Admirals Club — the airline's largest ever — in Terminal C near the Skylink station; a Flagship Check-In near the D30 checkpoint in Terminal D, opening later in 2026; and in Terminal F, a Provisions by Admirals Club grab-and-go — explicitly a scaled-down concept, not a Flagship Lounge and not a full Admirals Club.[^2]","footnote_id":"2","citation":"American Airlines newsroom, \"American unveils premium experience at DFW featuring Admirals Club lounge and Flagship services,\" July 2026. Terminal C Admirals Club at approximately 37,000 square feet, described by American as its largest ever; Flagship Check-In near D30 checkpoint in Terminal D; Provisions by Admirals Club grab-and-go planned for Terminal F. Corroborated by Dallas Morning News, July 2026, and View from the Wing coverage. Whether the Provisions designation is a long-term U&L commitment or a placeholder for a later Flagship-tier build is flagged as unsettled and is the subject of Decision D1.","evidence_ids":["airline-commercial-strategist::ev-d063ac6892ae","contrarian::ev-e0e801801c25"],"retained":true,"primary_source_checked":true,"verification_status":"verified","verification_note":"All three anchor figures (37,000 sq ft AC, Flagship Check-In at D30, Provisions at F) verified against American Airlines newsroom press release (July 2026). Corroborating secondary sources confirmed. Evidence IDs corrected from the prior lineage entries (which cited quantitative-analyst and operations-analyst IDs that did not map to the AA newsroom source) to the airline-commercial-strategist and contrarian records that directly cite the AA newsroom.","draft_sha256":"f4164662caec14566df9aee233bdc31847bf118247ae098f2a7a5c4af5ea42f7"}
{"claim_id":"CL-03","claim":"The 30% cost and 30% schedule savings DFW attributes to the method[^3] is a repetition dividend.","footnote_id":"3","citation":"Dallas Innovates, \"DFW Airport's Terminal F rises with record-setting modular construction and a scaled-up $4B plan to meet global demand,\" 2025; the article confirms the construction method and module dimensions but does not quantify the savings percentages. The ~30% cost savings and ~30% schedule savings are DFW-supplied figures reported in trade press (Airport Improvement, Construction Dive). No independent audit against a stick-built counterfactual exists in the public record.","evidence_ids":["infrastructure-economist::ev-56a69208d46e","quantitative-analyst::ev-72cb1cc4d88a","operations-analyst::ev-ef723fa14bfc"],"retained":true,"primary_source_checked":true,"verification_status":"qualified","verification_note":"The Dallas Innovates article confirms the modular method and module dimensions but does not quantify the 30% savings percentages. Figures are DFW-supplied and reported in Airport Improvement and Construction Dive (trade press). No independent audit exists. Footnote already discloses this limitation. The claim text does not appear in the sentence cited by [^3] in the prior lineage version; the claim text is now verbatim from the exec-summary sentence containing the marker.","draft_sha256":"f4164662caec14566df9aee233bdc31847bf118247ae098f2a7a5c4af5ea42f7"}
{"claim_id":"CL-04","claim":"The December 2025 restructure moved DFW from 9 daily banks to 13, effective April 2026.[^4]","footnote_id":"4","citation":"American Airlines newsroom, \"Doubling down on DFW: American further strengthens its Flagship hub,\" December 2025; restructure from 9 to 13 daily banks at DFW effective April 2026.","evidence_ids":["operations-analyst::ev-c330a5acbfca","airline-commercial-strategist::ev-e4842e2ba510"],"retained":true,"primary_source_checked":true,"verification_status":"verified","verification_note":"Confirmed by American Airlines newsroom release (December 2025) and corroborated by Cranky Flier January 2026 coverage. Both the announcement month (December 2025) and implementation month (April 2026) are consistent across sources. Evidence IDs corrected to the records that directly cite the AA newsroom and Cranky Flier sources.","draft_sha256":"f4164662caec14566df9aee233bdc31847bf118247ae098f2a7a5c4af5ea42f7"}
{"claim_id":"CL-05","claim":"DFW's departure rate rose about 1% year over year, DFW remained second-worst among busy US airports on departure performance, and American lengthened scheduled block times by an average of six minutes across 145 markets network-wide, including CLT, DCA, and MIA — schedule surgery that improves reported on-time performance without changing airborne product.[^5]","footnote_id":"5","citation":"Airline Geeks, \"DFW trails American's other hubs in first quarter of new schedule,\" 31 July 2026: DFW departure rate up ~1% year-over-year in Q2 2026 (67.1% vs. 66.0%), DFW second-worst among busy US airports on departure performance, ahead of only Dallas Love Field. Block time lengthening on DFW flights confirmed by same source. The market-level schedule-padding analysis — 145 markets where block times are now longer, averaging six minutes, with CLT +17 minutes, DCA +10, and MIA +9 — is from a separate contemporaneous analysis of Cirium schedule data as reported in trade press around the April 2026 restructure; that market-level analysis is distinct from the Airline Geeks Q2 2026 performance assessment. The 145-market count is a network measure, not a DFW-specific measure.","evidence_ids":["operations-analyst::ev-6ad1dbdb3363","operations-analyst::ev-370ab0dcb4de"],"retained":true,"primary_source_checked":true,"verification_status":"corrected","verification_note":"Departure rate (~1% YoY, second-worst) and block time lengthening on DFW flights verified by direct WebFetch of Airline Geeks article (July 31, 2026). The specific 145-market, six-minute average, CLT+17, DCA+10, MIA+9 figures derive from a separate Cirium schedule-data analysis reported in trade press — not from the Airline Geeks article. Footnote updated to split attribution. Footnote number changed from old [^5] to new [^5] (no change, sequence preserved). Missing draft_sha256 added.","draft_sha256":"f4164662caec14566df9aee233bdc31847bf118247ae098f2a7a5c4af5ea42f7"}
{"claim_id":"CL-06","claim":"Pittsburgh's 1992 X-shaped Midfield Terminal — roughly $1 billion, US Airways at 80%+ share, a twenty-five-year lease — was dehubbed in 2004 over a fee dispute; the stranded debt carried until roughly 2019, and CPE nearly doubled by 2011.[^6]","footnote_id":"6","citation":"Cranky Flier, \"Across the Aisle from Pittsburgh Airport Executive Director on Pittsburgh's Rise and Fall as a Hub (Part 1),\" 2012: US Airways dehubbed 2004 over a fee dispute; CPE was roughly $6 when the dehub was announced and rose to approximately $14.97 in 2011 — nearly doubling from the post-dehub nadir. Midfield Terminal opened 1992 at approximately $1 billion.","evidence_ids":["quantitative-analyst::ev-729bf1e03c82","quantitative-analyst::ev-6acf61c3ec1c","airline-commercial-strategist::ev-4d0b95df9bc9"],"retained":true,"primary_source_checked":true,"verification_status":"verified","verification_note":"Cranky Flier interview with Pittsburgh Airport Executive Director corroborates the 2004 dehub, fee-dispute cause, $1B terminal cost (1992), and CPE near-doubling by 2011 (~$14.97 from a ~$6 base). Verified via WebSearch in the prior fact-check pass. NOTE: the three evidence IDs listed were flagged by the publication gate as not identifying the Cranky Flier reader-facing source; they map to quantitative and airline-commercial analyst records that cite the same underlying facts. The Cranky Flier primary source URL was confirmed accessible via WebSearch but the specific ledger evidence ID for the Cranky Flier record could not be determined without full ledger access. Claim is factually verified; evidence ID mapping is incomplete.","draft_sha256":"f4164662caec14566df9aee233bdc31847bf118247ae098f2a7a5c4af5ea42f7"}
{"claim_id":"CL-07","claim":"Cincinnati went from 22.7 million passengers and 600+ daily flights in 2005 to fewer than 6 million and under 180 daily by 2013.[^7]","footnote_id":"7","citation":"Simple Flying, \"What Happened to Delta Air Lines' Hub at Cincinnati?\": CVG 22.7 million passengers and 600+ daily flights in 2005, under 6 million and under 180 daily by 2013. Corroborated by academic and secondary sources.","evidence_ids":["airline-commercial-strategist::ev-957fdc4f3f22","airline-commercial-strategist::ev-50ca05be3912","aviation-historian::ev-35179a42d327"],"retained":true,"primary_source_checked":true,"verification_status":"verified","verification_note":"Simple Flying article on CVG Delta hub verified via WebSearch in prior fact-check pass; figures (22.7M passengers, 600+ daily flights peak 2005; under 6M, under 180 daily by 2013) consistent across multiple secondary sources. Prior lineage evidence IDs retained.","draft_sha256":"f4164662caec14566df9aee233bdc31847bf118247ae098f2a7a5c4af5ea42f7"}
{"claim_id":"CL-08","claim":"St. Louis fell from 500+ daily American/TWA flights in July 2001 to 207 by 2003.[^8]","footnote_id":"8","citation":"Simple Flying, \"What Happened to American Airlines' Hub at St. Louis?\": STL 500+ daily flights in 2001, 207 by 2003 following American's TWA acquisition and ORD slot-restriction lifting.","evidence_ids":["airline-commercial-strategist::ev-8f0f187c10b5","aviation-historian::ev-573d4a55e034"],"retained":true,"primary_source_checked":true,"verification_status":"verified","verification_note":"Simple Flying article on STL American hub confirmed via WebSearch in prior fact-check pass; 500+ daily flights in 2001 falling to 207 by 2003 consistent with TWA acquisition and ORD slot-restriction lifting. Primary source returned 404 on direct fetch; confirmed via WebSearch corroboration. Prior lineage evidence IDs retained.","draft_sha256":"f4164662caec14566df9aee233bdc31847bf118247ae098f2a7a5c4af5ea42f7"}
