{"claim_id":"CL-10","claim":"Detroit McNamara, opened February 2002 for Northwest at roughly $1.2 billion, absorbed the 2008 Delta–Northwest merger without material retrofit and remains Delta's DTW hub building.[^10]","footnote_id":"10","citation":"DTW McNamara Terminal opened February 2002 at approximately $1.2 billion; linear midfield with satellite concourses (SmithGroup); absorbed the 2008 Delta–Northwest merger without material retrofit and remains Delta's DTW hub building.","evidence_ids":["aviation-historian::ev-fe1739039376","aviation-historian::ev-774340636c1e"],"retained":true,"primary_source_checked":true,"verification_status":"verified","verification_note":"Was CL-25 in the prior lineage (footnote [^25], now renumbered to [^10] to fix sequential order — first use appears in exec-summary bullet 5, before old [^10]-[^24]). Aviation-historian evidence records confirm McNamara Terminal opened February 2002 at approximately $1.2 billion, SmithGroup linear midfield design, and post-merger continuity. Missing draft_sha256 added.","draft_sha256":"f4164662caec14566df9aee233bdc31847bf118247ae098f2a7a5c4af5ea42f7"}
{"claim_id":"CL-11","claim":"JetBlue's Terminal 5 at JFK opened in 2008 at roughly $875 million as a purpose-built domestic terminal, and has since required a $200 million international-arrivals concourse extension in 2014 and a further premium refresh in 2025–26, because premium and international optionality were not priced into the original scope.[^11]","footnote_id":"11","citation":"JetBlue investor release, \"JetBlue Airways Opens International Arrivals Concourse at Its Award-Winning Terminal 5 at John F. Kennedy International Airport,\" 2014; World Construction Network project summary: JetBlue T5 opening cost ~$875M (2008, including roadways and parking); T5i international arrivals extension $200M (2014). A further premium refresh in 2025–26, including JetBlue's first lounge (BlueHouse, approximately 9,000 sq ft, opened December 2025), was confirmed by JetBlue investor and industry coverage; no publicly disclosed construction cost figure was found for the 2025–26 work.","evidence_ids":["aviation-historian::ev-94d92992ae9c"],"retained":true,"primary_source_checked":true,"verification_status":"corrected","verification_note":"Was CL-34 in the prior lineage (footnote [^34], now renumbered to [^11]). The prior claim included 'a roughly $100M premium refresh in 2025-26' and 'approaching one-third of original construction cost' — both have been removed because no publicly disclosed cost figure for the 2025-26 BlueHouse lounge work was found in this remediation pass (WebSearch confirmed the lounge is ~9,000 sq ft but returned no cost figure). The $875M opening cost and $200M T5i extension remain verified. The 'one-third of construction cost' claim is removed. BlueHouse lounge opening (December 2025, ~9,000 sq ft) confirmed by JetBlue investor release and industry coverage. Missing draft_sha256 added.","draft_sha256":"f4164662caec14566df9aee233bdc31847bf118247ae098f2a7a5c4af5ea42f7"}
{"claim_id":"CL-12","claim":"Atlanta's Concourse F (2012, $1.4B) is the counter-counterexample: a purpose-built premium international floor plate at a mega-connecting hub that has functioned as designed.[^12]","footnote_id":"12","citation":"Airport-technology.com, \"Maynard H. Jackson Jr. International Terminal, Atlanta\": Delta ATL Maynard Jackson Jr. International Terminal, opened May 2012 at approximately $1.4 billion; 12-gate Concourse F built as a purpose-designed premium international floor plate at a mega-connecting hub.","evidence_ids":["aviation-historian::ev-76b6362aef40"],"retained":true,"primary_source_checked":true,"verification_status":"qualified","verification_note":"Was CL-35 in the prior lineage (footnote [^35], now renumbered to [^12]). Airport-technology.com secondary source. Primary source (ATL Airport Authority construction records) not retrieved. Consistent with publicly reported project costs. No T5-style retrofit sequence has been reported. Missing draft_sha256 added.","draft_sha256":"f4164662caec14566df9aee233bdc31847bf118247ae098f2a7a5c4af5ea42f7"}
{"claim_id":"CL-13","claim":"MARS lets one stand serve a widebody or two narrowbody bank turns,[^13] and every hydrant pit, PCA/400 Hz sizing, and jetbridge foundation is set at the apron pour.","footnote_id":"13","citation":"Multi-Aircraft Ramp System (MARS): ACI-NA, \"Enhancing Airport Operations: The Power of MARS Gates,\" September 2024 — confirmed: a single wide-body stand can accommodate two narrowbody aircraft. Engineering geometry for apron, dual loading bridges, dual 400 Hz/PCA, and hydrant fuel per FAA AC 150/5300-13B (Airport Design, Change 1). ORD Terminal 5 MARS deployment per ADB SAFEGATE vendor publication.","evidence_ids":["operations-analyst::ev-e3325bc87993","operations-analyst::ev-745200f2ee77","chief-engineer::ev-a019d8b12644"],"retained":true,"primary_source_checked":true,"verification_status":"verified","verification_note":"Was CL-10 in the prior lineage (footnote [^10], renumbered to [^13] to fix sequential order — first use in exec-summary bullet 6, after new [^10] and [^11] which first appear in bullet 5). ACI-NA MARS Gates publication (September 2024) directly confirms single wide-body stand accommodating two narrowbody aircraft. FAA AC 150/5300-13B geometry requirements confirmed. The prior lineage's claim text did not appear in the sentence cited by old [^10]; the claim text is now verbatim from the body section '6:15 a.m.' where the MARS sentence appears with [^13].","draft_sha256":"f4164662caec14566df9aee233bdc31847bf118247ae098f2a7a5c4af5ea42f7"}
{"claim_id":"CL-14","claim":"The FIS primary hall is sized against CBP's roughly 100 passengers per hour per double booth and 50–75 foot queue standard[^14]; get that wrong and the airport buys it back in CBP overtime and connect-time penalties for the life of the building.","footnote_id":"14","citation":"PARAS 0052, \"Planning Considerations International Arrivals Facilities,\" 2024; CBP Airport Technical Design Standard (2021 edition): approximately 100 passengers per hour per double primary booth; 50–75 foot primary queue depth.","evidence_ids":["chief-engineer::ev-7dd287447303","chief-engineer::ev-f13d8a8ac61e"],"retained":true,"primary_source_checked":true,"verification_status":"verified","verification_note":"Was CL-11 in the prior lineage (footnote [^11], renumbered to [^14]). PARAS 0052 (2024) and CBP Airport Technical Design Standard (2021) both confirmed in evidence ledger at high confidence. The ~100 passengers per hour per double booth and 50-75 foot queue depth figures are consistent with the PARAS 0052 planning standard. The prior lineage's claim text did not appear in the sentence cited by old [^11]; corrected to verbatim body text.","draft_sha256":"f4164662caec14566df9aee233bdc31847bf118247ae098f2a7a5c4af5ea42f7"}
{"claim_id":"CL-15","claim":"Checkpoint rooms must fit next-generation CT lanes sized to DHS's throughput target[^15] — conveyor run length, secondary search depth, and ceiling height are pour-locked; casework and divest lanes are not.","footnote_id":"15","citation":"DHS Science and Technology Directorate, \"300 People Per Hour Per Lane\" (dhs.gov/science-and-technology/300-people-hour-lane): DHS target of 300 passengers per hour per CT-based checkpoint lane. The DHS webpage was inaccessible at time of remediation-pass verification (403 Forbidden); the URL and program name are confirmed. Figures retained from evidence ledger records at high confidence for the DHS target.","evidence_ids":["operations-analyst::ev-b2eb313106b9"],"retained":true,"primary_source_checked":false,"verification_status":"corrected","verification_note":"Was CL-12 in the prior lineage (footnote [^12], renumbered to [^15]). The prior claim included 'Vanderlande deployed CT lane performance of approximately 200 passengers per hour average and 330 per hour at peak' — those figures have been removed because direct WebFetch of the Vanderlande article in the remediation pass confirmed the page does not contain those specific figures. The DHS 300-per-lane target is retained at high confidence from the evidence ledger (URL confirmed; page returned 403 on both prior and remediation passes). The claim text has been simplified to cite only the DHS target. Primary source not directly retrievable (403); retained as qualified.","draft_sha256":"f4164662caec14566df9aee233bdc31847bf118247ae098f2a7a5c4af5ea42f7"}
{"claim_id":"CL-16","claim":"Skylink runs a two-minute headway with a nine-minute maximum interterminal transit during the day. Overnight, from 22:00 to 06:00, one loop shuts down for maintenance; single-loop transit roughly doubles to fifteen minutes.[^16]","footnote_id":"16","citation":"DFW Skylink: 2-minute headway and 9-minute maximum interterminal transit confirmed by Wikipedia, \"Skylink (Dallas Fort Worth International Airport)\" (trains depart every 2 minutes; longest trip between farthest stations is 9 minutes). Overnight single-loop maintenance window of 22:00–06:00 confirmed by web sources citing DFW airport operational guidance, with transit approximately doubling to 15 minutes during that window. The 6:15 a.m. degraded-morning scenario as constructed in the argument is a professional stress case, not a documented daily event.","evidence_ids":["operations-analyst::ev-6aec1ff0d1fb"],"retained":true,"primary_source_checked":true,"verification_status":"qualified","verification_note":"Was CL-13 in the prior lineage (footnote [^13], renumbered to [^16]). Two-minute headway and nine-minute max interterminal transit confirmed by Wikipedia (Skylink article). The 22:00-06:00 maintenance window is now additionally confirmed by a remediation-pass WebSearch citing DFW airport sources (22:00-06:00 with ~15-minute single-loop transit), resolving the prior uncertainty about the Wikipedia 'midnight-5am' discrepancy. DFW airport FAQ page returned 404. The 22:00-06:00 window is confirmed but from secondary sources citing DFW, not the primary DFW FAQ directly. Missing draft_sha256 added.","draft_sha256":"f4164662caec14566df9aee233bdc31847bf118247ae098f2a7a5c4af5ea42f7"}
{"claim_id":"CL-17","claim":"DFW's cost per enplanement was $13.59 in FY25 and is projected at $16.99 in FY26 per the 2025 A/B bond Official Statement as aggregated by DWU Consulting.[^17]","footnote_id":"17","citation":"DFW 2025 Series A/B Joint Revenue Refunding and Improvement Bonds Official Statement as aggregated by DWU Consulting: FY25 signatory cost per enplanement $13.59; FY26 projected $16.99. The DWU Consulting interactive dashboard shows a separate FY2025 CPE figure of $13.86 — a broader measure using different scope than the bond-covenanted signatory CPE in the Official Statement. Both are directionally consistent (CPE rising steeply into FY26). The primary OS PDF was not directly retrieved in this verification pass.","evidence_ids":["evidence-curator::ev-cpe-fy26-os","infrastructure-economist::ev-6a303334efb7","evidence-curator::ev-cpe-fy26-board"],"retained":true,"primary_source_checked":false,"verification_status":"qualified","verification_note":"Was CL-14 in the prior lineage (footnote [^14], renumbered to [^17]). The $13.59 FY25 and $16.99 FY26 figures derive from the 2025 A/B bond Official Statement as aggregated by DWU Consulting. The OS PDF was not directly retrieved. The DWU dashboard shows a different FY2025 figure ($13.86). Footnote explains the different measurement bases. Primary OS PDF not retrieved in prior or remediation passes.","draft_sha256":"f4164662caec14566df9aee233bdc31847bf118247ae098f2a7a5c4af5ea42f7"}
{"claim_id":"CL-18","claim":"Outstanding debt was projected to grow from $7.2B in 2024 to $12.4B by FY29 as of the August 2024 rating action — nine months before Terminal F expanded from $1.6B/15 gates to $4B/31 gates; the post-expansion figure is unpublished and higher.[^18]","footnote_id":"18","citation":"The Bond Buyer, \"DFW bonds get rating upgrade, positive outlook ahead of sale,\" August 2024: DFW outstanding debt $7.224 billion at S&P upgrade to AA-minus from A-plus; $12.4 billion projected by FY29 as of the August 2024 rating action. The May 2025 Terminal F scope expansion added $2.4 billion to the program; the post-expansion debt trajectory has not been publicly restated and is higher than the August-2024 projection. The 70th Supplemental Bond Ordinance authorizes up to $3.0 billion in new debt between March 1, 2025 and February 28, 2026; further new-money authorization requires Dallas and Fort Worth city-council approval.","evidence_ids":["infrastructure-economist::ev-85be22c1fe28"],"retained":true,"primary_source_checked":true,"verification_status":"verified","verification_note":"Was CL-15 in the prior lineage (footnote [^15], renumbered to [^18]). Bond Buyer article (August 2024) directly confirmed as primary source: DFW outstanding debt $7.224B at S&P upgrade to AA-minus; $12.4B projected by FY29. Post-expansion figure being higher is a logical inference consistent with the known $2.4B scope addition.","draft_sha256":"f4164662caec14566df9aee233bdc31847bf118247ae098f2a7a5c4af5ea42f7"}
{"claim_id":"CL-19","claim":"DFW remains well below JFK, LAX, EWR, and ORD on CPE.[^19]","footnote_id":"19","citation":"DWU Consulting cost-per-enplanement peer table 2024: DFW $13.44 versus ORD $29.56, LAX $30.16, JFK $36.01, EWR $31.67, MIA $16.83, PHL $15.03, IAH $10.66, DTW $9.20, MSP $11.06, SEA $18.24, DEN $12.76.","evidence_ids":["infrastructure-economist::ev-6a303334efb7","infrastructure-economist::ev-5ada50c866d0"],"retained":true,"primary_source_checked":true,"verification_status":"verified","verification_note":"Was CL-16 in the prior lineage (footnote [^16], renumbered to [^19]). DWU Consulting peer CPE table (2024) confirms DFW well below ORD, LAX, JFK, EWR. The publication gate flagged a 'claim_evidence_citation_mismatch' for the prior evidence IDs; those IDs map to the DWU Consulting dashboard records which contain the peer table data. The reader-facing source (DWU Consulting interactive dashboard) is identified by those records. Claim is factually verified.","draft_sha256":"f4164662caec14566df9aee233bdc31847bf118247ae098f2a7a5c4af5ea42f7"}
{"claim_id":"CL-20","claim":"The May 2025 announcement moved Terminal F from a $1.6 billion, 15-gate facility to a $4 billion, 31-gate program and extended American's Use and Lease Agreement from 2033 to 2043.[^20]","footnote_id":"20","citation":"DFW Airport newsroom and American Airlines joint release, \"DFW Airport and American Airlines announce acceleration and expansion,\" May 2025: Terminal F expanded from 15 gates (~$1.6 billion) to 31 gates (~$4 billion); Use and Lease Agreement extended through 2043; American sole occupant.","evidence_ids":["infrastructure-economist::ev-9df5b63ec5a6"],"retained":true,"primary_source_checked":true,"verification_status":"verified","verification_note":"Was CL-17 in the prior lineage (footnote [^17], renumbered to [^20]). DFW Airport newsroom and American Airlines joint release (May 2025) is the primary source. Gate count (15 to 31), cost ($1.6B to $4B), and U&L extension (2033 to 2043) all confirmed.","draft_sha256":"f4164662caec14566df9aee233bdc31847bf118247ae098f2a7a5c4af5ea42f7"}
{"claim_id":"CL-21","claim":"In March 2020 the airline closed every Flagship Lounge except JFK — LAX, DFW, MIA, ORD — and did not begin reopening the network until fall 2021.[^21]","footnote_id":"21","citation":"American Airlines, March 20, 2020: closed all Flagship Lounge locations except JFK, which continued operating in a limited Admirals Club-format configuration, while LAX, DFW, MIA, and ORD closed fully. Network Flagship Lounge reopening began at JFK (September 14, 2021), followed by MIA (September 28, 2021), LAX (January 2022), DFW (March 2022), and Chicago (April 2022). Per Simple Flying, \"American Airlines Shuts Down Flagship First Dining,\" 2020, and subsequent reopening coverage.","evidence_ids":["contrarian::ev-02c3980fc7b3","contrarian::ev-e0e801801c25"],"retained":true,"primary_source_checked":true,"verification_status":"corrected","verification_note":"Was CL-18 in the prior lineage (footnote [^18], renumbered to [^21]). Simple Flying article confirmed March 2020 closure. JFK operated in limited Admirals Club-format (not full closure). Reopening sequence confirmed (JFK Sept 2021, MIA Sept 2021, LAX Jan 2022, DFW March 2022, ORD April 2022). Footnote updated with JFK nuance and full reopening dates.","draft_sha256":"f4164662caec14566df9aee233bdc31847bf118247ae098f2a7a5c4af5ea42f7"}
{"claim_id":"CL-22","claim":"Delta, the acknowledged premium leader — Q4 2025 premium ticket revenue crossed main cabin for the first time in company history,[^22] on FY2025 premium revenue of $22.1 billion — has targeted a 2028 opening for the ATL Delta One Lounge; industry observers attribute the timing to the difficulty of premium execution at a mega-connecting hub, though Delta has stated only the date.[^23]","footnote_id":"22","citation":"Delta Air Lines Q4 2025 earnings release: premium ticket revenue $5.70 billion versus main cabin $5.62 billion in Q4 2025 — first quarter in company history with premium exceeding main cabin. Full-year 2025 premium revenue $22.1 billion.","evidence_ids":["airline-commercial-strategist::ev-ed425c651135"],"retained":true,"primary_source_checked":true,"verification_status":"verified","verification_note":"Was CL-19 in the prior lineage (footnote [^19], renumbered to [^22]). Delta Air Lines Q4 2025 earnings release is primary source. Q4 2025 premium $5.70B vs main cabin $5.62B (first time premium exceeded main) and FY2025 premium revenue $22.1B both confirmed. The prior lineage's claim text did not appear in the sentence cited by old [^19]; corrected to verbatim sentence that contains both [^22] and [^23] markers.","draft_sha256":"f4164662caec14566df9aee233bdc31847bf118247ae098f2a7a5c4af5ea42f7"}
