{"claim_id":"CL-25","claim":"American's targets — narrowbody premium share from ~25% to ~40%, international lie-flat count up more than 50% by end of decade[^24] — are real, and the airline is shifting long-haul weight into DFW: 6% year-over-year Q3 2026 long-haul capacity growth alongside a 13% reduction at Heathrow, plus six new international routes for summer 2026.[^25]","footnote_id":"25","citation":"Simple Flying / Travel and Tour World, analysis of American Airlines long-haul capacity data: DFW +6% Q3 2026 long-haul departures year-over-year versus LHR –13%. Six new international routes from DFW for summer 2026 per American Airlines newsroom via aviationa2z (DFW–Budapest, Prague, Athens, Milan, Zurich, plus expanded DFW–Buenos Aires).","evidence_ids":["airline-commercial-strategist::ev-5b76a2fb6b0f","operations-analyst::ev-6ad1dbdb3363"],"retained":true,"primary_source_checked":true,"verification_status":"verified","verification_note":"Was CL-22 in the prior lineage (footnote [^22], renumbered to [^25]). Simple Flying/Travel and Tour World analysis of AA long-haul capacity data confirms DFW +6% Q3 2026 YoY and LHR -13%. Six new international routes for summer 2026 confirmed by AA newsroom. The prior lineage's claim text did not appear verbatim in the sentence cited by old [^22]; corrected to the full sentence containing [^25]. Missing draft_sha256 added.","draft_sha256":"f4164662caec14566df9aee233bdc31847bf118247ae098f2a7a5c4af5ea42f7"}
{"claim_id":"CL-26","claim":"American's own initial data for its first seventeen days showed a 50% reduction in missed connections[^26] — the airline's own figure, from an employee briefing in a short initial window.","footnote_id":"26","citation":"American Airlines \"Forever Forward at DFW: Enhanced flight schedule unlocks countless customer benefits\" newsroom release, April 2026: first two weeks of the 13-bank schedule described as delivering strong results with qualitative performance improvements. The 50% missed-connections reduction — covering the first seventeen days of operation (April 7–23, 2026) compared with the same period in 2025 — was reported by DFW hub head Jim Moses at an internal employee meeting; per View from the Wing, \"American Airlines Rebuilt Its Dallas Hub — Missed Connections Are Down 50%,\" 2026, reviewing a recording of that meeting. The figure is AA's own internal data for a short initial window, not independently audited.","evidence_ids":[],"retained":true,"primary_source_checked":true,"verification_status":"verified","verification_note":"Was CL-36 in the prior lineage (no footnote; evidence_ids empty; primary_source_checked: false). In the remediation pass, WebFetch of the View from the Wing article confirmed the 50% figure as Jim Moses's statement at an internal AA employee meeting covering the first 17 days of the 13-bank schedule (April 7–23, 2026). The AA newsroom 'Forever Forward' release confirms the two-week timeframe and qualitative results but does not cite the 50% figure directly; that specific percentage is from the employee briefing as reported by View from the Wing. Footnote [^26] added. Draft appropriately qualifies as 'the airline's own figure, from an employee briefing in a short initial window.' No ledger evidence ID exists for this source; verified directly against web source in this remediation pass.","draft_sha256":"f4164662caec14566df9aee233bdc31847bf118247ae098f2a7a5c4af5ea42f7"}
