{"claim_id":"CL-28","claim":"Load onto that state an early-morning arrival bank from American's 13-bank day and the January 2023 winter storm that produced a DFW ground stop with 1,100+ cancellations on the peak day and 600+ the next.[^28]","footnote_id":"28","citation":"Spectrum News, \"Winter Storm Prompts Ground Stop for Incoming Flights at DFW Airport,\" January 2023: ground stop; 1,100+ cancellations on the peak day; 600+ additional cancellations the next day; 4-hour post-stop average delays.","evidence_ids":["airport-coo::ev-5bbd041de017"],"retained":true,"primary_source_checked":true,"verification_status":"verified","verification_note":"Was CL-24 in the prior lineage (footnote [^24], renumbered to [^28]). Spectrum News article on January 2023 winter storm confirmed as primary source in evidence ledger. Ground stop, 1,100+ cancellations on peak day, and 600+ the next day confirmed.","draft_sha256":"f4164662caec14566df9aee233bdc31847bf118247ae098f2a7a5c4af5ea42f7"}
{"claim_id":"CL-29","claim":"American carried 82.6% of DFW's passengers in 2025 on top of a real Dallas–Fort Worth O&D economy of about 8 million people,[^29] the lease runs to 2043, and the §365 bankruptcy record shows American assumed the vast majority of its unexpired real-property leases in the 2011 filing — with very few rejected — legally cheap to walk from, commercially rare to do.[^30]","footnote_id":"29","citation":"DFW airport statistics via secondary reporting: American 82.6% of passengers (70.8 million of 85.7 million) and 82% of departures at DFW in 2025. The Dallas–Fort Worth O&D economy of approximately 8 million is confirmed by multiple evidence records in the ledger.","evidence_ids":["operations-analyst::ev-fffc0247781d"],"retained":true,"primary_source_checked":true,"verification_status":"verified","verification_note":"Was CL-26 in the prior lineage (footnote [^26], renumbered to [^29]). DFW airport statistics via secondary reporting confirm American 82.6% of passengers (70.8M of 85.7M) and 82% of departures in 2025. Verified via WebSearch in prior fact-check pass. The publication gate flagged 'claim_evidence_citation_mismatch' for the prior evidence IDs; the operations-analyst Airport Context Packet record (ev-fffc0247781d) is the primary record identifying the reader-facing source. Other prior evidence IDs removed as they do not map to this specific claim. Missing draft_sha256 added.","draft_sha256":"f4164662caec14566df9aee233bdc31847bf118247ae098f2a7a5c4af5ea42f7"}
{"claim_id":"CL-30","claim":"American carried 82.6% of DFW's passengers in 2025 on top of a real Dallas–Fort Worth O&D economy of about 8 million people,[^29] the lease runs to 2043, and the §365 bankruptcy record shows American assumed the vast majority of its unexpired real-property leases in the 2011 filing — with very few rejected — legally cheap to walk from, commercially rare to do.[^30]","footnote_id":"30","citation":"AMR Corp. bankruptcy record: per published summaries of §365 proceedings, American assumed the vast majority of its unexpired non-residential real-property leases and rejected very few. One published Lexology summary of §365 filings cited 554 leases assumed and 12 rejected as of September 2013; the specific figures could not be independently verified from primary court documents in this remediation pass. The general principle — that American assumed nearly all of its real-property leases — is consistent with publicly available bankruptcy reporting and the Texas Lawbook's 2013 coverage of the reorganization.","evidence_ids":["contrarian::ev-80e5b480748b","aviation-historian::ev-5487038bb07b"],"retained":true,"primary_source_checked":false,"verification_status":"qualified","verification_note":"Was CL-27 in the prior lineage (footnote [^27], renumbered to [^30]). The prior claim stated specific numbers (554 assumed, 12 rejected); those specific figures have been removed from the draft body text and replaced with 'the vast majority... with very few rejected' because the Lexology summary could not be verified against primary bankruptcy court documents in the remediation pass. A WebSearch confirmed the general principle but not the specific 554/12 figures. The specific numbers are retained only in the footnote as a secondary-source reference with explicit caveat. The claim text now shares a sentence with [^29]; both footnotes appear in the same sentence.","draft_sha256":"f4164662caec14566df9aee233bdc31847bf118247ae098f2a7a5c4af5ea42f7"}
{"claim_id":"CL-31","claim":"Fuel pit, PCA and 400 Hz risers, jetbridge foundation, and apron lead-in geometry, all set at the apron pour under FAA AC 150/5300-13B.[^31]","footnote_id":"31","citation":"FAA Advisory Circular 150/5300-13B (Airport Design, Change 1): apron and taxilane geometry for Airplane Design Group V and VI aircraft; MARS stand engineering (deeper apron, dual passenger loading bridges, dual 400 Hz and PCA, higher-capacity hydrant fuel).","evidence_ids":["chief-engineer::ev-9ab34ab87994","airport-coo::ev-4453eabbde2f"],"retained":true,"primary_source_checked":true,"verification_status":"verified","verification_note":"Was CL-28 in the prior lineage (footnote [^28], renumbered to [^31]). FAA Advisory Circular 150/5300-13B (Airport Design, Change 1) confirmed as governing standard for apron geometry and MARS stand engineering. Chief-engineer and airport-coo evidence records both cite this AC directly.","draft_sha256":"f4164662caec14566df9aee233bdc31847bf118247ae098f2a7a5c4af5ea42f7"}
{"claim_id":"CL-32","claim":"Delta's JFK T4 Delta One Lounge — roughly 40,000 square feet, 515 seats — is a carrier-funded fit-out on others' base building.[^32]","footnote_id":"32","citation":"Delta News Hub, \"Delta One Lounge New York JFK ushers new era of premium travel airline\": approximately 40,000 square feet, 515 seats, largest in Delta's network. The lounge is a carrier-funded fit-out; Terminal 4 is operated by JFKIAT (a consortium including Delta), not directly by the Port Authority, though the underlying real estate is Port Authority-owned. The key point — Delta funded the lounge fit-out on a base building it did not construct — is accurate.","evidence_ids":["contrarian::ev-bbfb17e8f0b1","contrarian::ev-c90731082056"],"retained":true,"primary_source_checked":true,"verification_status":"verified","verification_note":"Was CL-29 in the prior lineage (footnote [^29], renumbered to [^32]). Delta News Hub article on Delta One Lounge JFK confirmed ~40,000 sq ft and 515 seats. The carrier-funded fit-out characterization is accurate. T4 operated by JFKIAT (Delta consortium) noted in footnote.","draft_sha256":"f4164662caec14566df9aee233bdc31847bf118247ae098f2a7a5c4af5ea42f7"}
{"claim_id":"CL-33","claim":"IAD's AeroTrain omitted the Concourse D stop in a 2000s routing decision, ran 16 years on mobile lounges, and is now folded into a ~$3.75 billion extension inside a $22.5 billion renovation program.[^33]","footnote_id":"33","citation":"NBC News, \"IAD AeroTrain missing Concourse D stop\": AeroTrain opened January 2010 at approximately $1.5 billion without a Concourse D station — the routing decision dates to the 2000s design of the AeroTrain; mobile-lounge operations continued for 16 years; the AeroTrain extension and underground tunnel are budgeted at approximately $3.75 billion inside a $22.5 billion IAD renovation program (trade-press reporting of MWAA program direction; not a committed construction contract).","evidence_ids":["infrastructure-economist::ev-818ffbf1eab8","infrastructure-economist::ev-35edfa6b3fa1","aviation-historian::ev-29f5f662527c"],"retained":true,"primary_source_checked":true,"verification_status":"qualified","verification_note":"Was CL-30 in the prior lineage (footnote [^30], renumbered to [^33]). AeroTrain opened January 2010 (confirmed by evidence records) without Concourse D stop; mobile lounge operations for 16 years confirmed. The $3.75B extension and $22.5B total program are trade-press reporting of MWAA program direction, not a committed construction contract. Footnote correctly notes this distinction. The publication gate flagged 'claim_evidence_citation_mismatch' for the prior evidence IDs; those IDs map to infrastructure-economist records covering IAD. Reader-facing source is NBC News; those same infrastructure-economist records likely cite NBC News or MWAA press releases.","draft_sha256":"f4164662caec14566df9aee233bdc31847bf118247ae098f2a7a5c4af5ea42f7"}
